---
import InsertLayout from '../layouts/InsertLayout.astro';
import { breadcrumbSchema } from '../utils/jsonld';

const jsonLd = breadcrumbSchema([
  { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
  { name: 'About', url: '/about' }
]);
---

<InsertLayout
  title="About"
  description="How Nicholas Toledo runs Toledo Technologies: a founder-led software practice with direct technical accountability."
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  heading="The Practice"
  subheading="Founder-led software work, with the actual author visible."
>
  <p>
    Toledo Technologies is Nicholas Toledo's software practice. On a standard
    engagement, Nicholas handles the first conversation, architecture, and
    implementation. If a specialist or collaborator is needed, that person and
    their role are disclosed before their work enters the project.
  </p>

  <p>
    That structure keeps responsibility close to the code. It does not imply a
    large hidden studio, an anonymous bench of engineers, or client history that
    cannot be traced to a named engagement.
  </p>

  <h2>The Model</h2>

  <p>
    The practice is intentionally direct: fewer handoffs, written decisions, and
    a clear owner for the technical result. Projects that need a large delivery
    organization or a permanent multi-team staffing model are not a fit.
    Projects where a senior builder can understand the system end to end often
    are.
  </p>

  <p>
    Discovery is paid because uncertainty is work. It turns business goals,
    constraints, and risks into a decision record, technical plan, scope, and
    quote basis. When a qualifying project starts within 30 days, 50% of the
    discovery fee is credited to the kickoff Project Retainer. The credit is
    capped at that Project Retainer; unused credit lapses and does not roll
    forward.
  </p>

  <h2>What High-Fidelity Means in Code</h2>

  <p>
    High-fidelity software preserves the useful intent of the business without
    adding avoidable complexity. The work favors legible systems, tests around
    critical paths, documented decisions, and infrastructure the owner can
    actually control.
  </p>

  <p>
    The goal is not a theatrical process. It is a working system whose limits,
    operating requirements, and handoff state are understandable to the next
    person who touches it.
  </p>

  <h2>The Three Rules</h2>

  <p>
    <strong>1. Scope follows evidence.</strong> Material unknowns are resolved before
    a fixed implementation scope is promised. Estimates and assumptions stay visible.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>2. Ownership is written down.</strong> Repository access, infrastructure,
    credentials, third-party accounts, and intellectual-property terms are defined
    in the project agreement. No website sentence replaces that agreement.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>3. Claims keep their provenance.</strong> A reference build is not called
    a client deployment. Independent research is not called a private audit. A sample
    is labeled as a sample.
  </p>

  <h2>The Archives</h2>

  <p>
    Public repositories span different stages of maturity: experiments, tools,
    reference systems, and maintained applications. Their existence is useful
    evidence of technical range, but it is not a blanket claim that every
    repository is production-deployed or client-owned.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a href="/portfolio">Read the labeled proof ledger →</a><br />
    <a
      href="https://github.com/ntoledo319"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer"
    >
      Inspect the public GitHub profile →
    </a>
  </p>

  <hr />

  <p class="pull">
    One accountable builder, a visible chain of evidence, and a scope that says
    what the work actually is.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a href="/contact?subject=Project%20inquiry">Write the practice →</a>
  </p>
</InsertLayout>
